  "query_preview": "..High pressure over the area will shift eastward for\nFriday, allowing a south to southwesterly flow to return to the\narea. This will spread moisture back throughout Utah. PWATs this \nevening range from 0.8-0.9 inches across northern Utah to 1.1 \ninches across southern Utah, and will increase to around 1 inch \nacross the north to 1.3 inches across southwest Utah by late \nFriday afternoon. Expect this to bring increasing coverage of\nshowers and thunderstorms throughout the forecast area for Friday\nafternoon and evening, with an increased potential for flash \nflooding. A Marginal Risk (level 1/4) for excessive rainfall\ncontinues for portions of southern Utah for Friday.\n\nThe flow turns a bit more westerly for the weekend, which will\nlead to a decreasing trend in PWATs. Even so, enough moisture will\nlinger to produce isolated to scattered convection, with an\nassociated decrease in flash flood potential. Another moisture\nsurge looks to be in store for the area during the early to middle\nportion of next week, before another drying trend towards the end\nof week. As such, expect convection coverage to increase and \ndecrease with the moisture.\n\nIn addition to a return to wetter conditions, this pattern\nwill bring an increase in temperatures especially across northern\nUtah Friday into the weekend as the ridge not only shifts but\nH5 heights continue to climb as the ridge amplifies. Daytime \nhighs soar back to the century mark for many locations across the \nWasatch Front, with overnight lows in the mid to upper 70s \noffering very little relief. As such, increasingly widespread \nMajor HeatRisk emerges through this period, indicating an elevated\nthreat for heat illness especially for those without adequate \ncooling and hydration. Areas where cloud cover and remnant surface\nmoisture will see the least overnight cooling, and there is \nindication that some areas with lows sitting around 80F could \nreach Extreme HeatRisk (~20% chance). If outdoors for festivities \nthis weekend, make sure to stay hydrated and take breaks in the \nshade or air conditioning often.",
